WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.889
Film Courage: So let’s say someone has made
a bold move come from wherever to Los Angeles

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:09.000
or New York (one of these major cities) and
they’ve been here several years and now

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.740
they’re in a new kind of rut, a new kind
of comfort zone….

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.740
“Yeah, I’m going out.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:20.400
I’m submitting [fill in the blank of whatever]
but it’s habit-driven and it’s not really

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.400
going anywhere.”

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:28.760
But hey, they are here in this big city and
are supposedly working, they are supposedly

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.439
getting stuff out there, how do you shake
that up?

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:33.439
And I know I’m being a little bit vague.

00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:34.439
Gary W. Goldstein: It’s not vague.

00:00:34.439 --> 00:00:36.300
Film Courage: But let’s just say they are
here.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:45.210
Gary W. Goldstein: If you want to play tennis,
find yourself a pro.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:46.739
That’s the game.

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:48.510
That’s how you improve.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:56.890
The problem with most creatives, they comes
to LA, the come to New York and what do they

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:57.890
do?

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.670
Most of them learn bad habits, why?

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:05.950
Because they are learning, mimicking like
people around them.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.579
I’m an actor, I’m following what other
actors are doing.

00:01:09.579 --> 00:01:14.680
I’m asking them, I’m learning from them,
yet they are not at the top of the totem.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.950
Same with writers, same with producers, it
doesn’t matter.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.439
Human beings.

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:30.369
Writers (as one example), I get query letters
every day, blind query letters.

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:33.840
“Dear So and So…”

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.749
It could be my name, it could be ‘creative
executive,’ it could be my name misspelled,

00:01:36.749 --> 00:01:37.749
it could be anything.

00:01:37.749 --> 00:01:38.749
“Dear So and So…”

00:01:38.749 --> 00:01:43.319
Log line, short description, “Can I send
a script?

00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:44.659
I’ll sign a release.”

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:46.409
Signed…John.

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:51.859
That’s what I get repeatedly all day long
in my inbox.

00:01:51.859 --> 00:01:54.319
There is not one lick of research.

00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:58.829
There is not one lick of anything personal,
like why is this the writer (the only writer)

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:00.069
who could have written this story?

00:02:00.069 --> 00:02:08.119
Why was it so compelling that they chose this
out of all the stories in one’s imagination?

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:09.780
Why did they choose to send it to me?

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.150
Now I don’t want to receive the agony and
the ecstasy as a letter but something…a

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:22.250
drop, a kernel, something that stands out
and makes me think.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.400
Makes me react.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.480
Makes me wonder about this.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.480
But they don’t.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.290
I can promise you not even 1% do what I’m
describing.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.209
So this is what writers learn from other writers
and the actors learning from other actors,

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:39.209
etc.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:48.090
They learn the habitual tried-and-true, day-to-day
protocol which is usually very unsuccessful.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.469
The ROI (the return-on-investment) in sending
blind query blind query letters has got to

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:52.469
be horribly frustrating?

00:02:52.469 --> 00:02:55.650
If you’re a business, you’d be bankrupt.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.150
So why do it?

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.379
Because this is what other people are doing.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:05.150
You need to associate with more successful
people and you need to think about it differently.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:10.629
If you were a fashion designer and you took
that approach, you focus on your craft, focus

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.230
on your craft, focus on your craft and you
sent out letters saying “Here’s my designs.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.270
Aren’t I great!”

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.390
You’d never have a brand.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.920
You have to think in terms of business.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.470
You have to think in terms of ‘What more
can I do that others aren’t doing?’

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.209
As opposed to ‘What they are doing.

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:29.900
How do I stand out?’

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:37.010
Separating yourself out from the herd is definitely
the name of the game and anyone who has invested

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:44.459
that blood, sweat and tears, that sort of
soul equity of investing all of that time

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:52.939
writing 120 or 110 pages (whatever) of story
and rewriting it until it is just so.

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:54.939
But they want to share their wares with the
world.

00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:56.730
Now they’re so proud of this.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:03.269
They should invest a like amount of time figuring
out how to market and how to be a business

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:07.810
person and how to reach out to people and
how to do things differently than all of their

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:08.810
peers.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:09.810
Like that’s a great measurement.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.800
Am I doing what everyone else is doing or
am I being somehow, putting myself out into

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:17.250
the world and speaking of myself to the right
people in ways that matter?

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.280
Film Courage: You mentioned fashion and it
had me thinking of [designer] Diane von Furstenberg

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.832
and her wrap dress and how it was this dress
that no one had ever really seen before and

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:28.210
it fit every body type.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:31.860
It made every body type look good and so you
are talking about fashion and it made me think

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:32.860
of that.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:39.300
Gary W. Goldstein: How many times do we see
artists like Steve Jobs, Johnny Cash, Lady

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.680
Gaga…I mean there’s a million ways to
stand out.

